# João: capítulo 7 (definições comuns das experiências) e capítulo 8 (resultados dos cenários já corridos).
# Números de data/processed/kpis_choque_*.xlsx e resultados_cenarios.xlsx (scripts/kpis_choque.py e resultados_cenarios.py).

$content['Experiments and Simulation Scenarios'] = {
    Body 'All experiments use the calibrated model of Section 6.3 with its parameters fixed. Each run covers 120 trading days of the real information of 2023–2024, from 13 June to 27 November 2024 (days 369 to 488 of the shock file, initial price 0.3494 EUR, the real close of 12 June 2024). In the shock scenarios a negative information shock of -10% hits the fundamental value at minute 30 600, the start of day 60 (5 September 2024). This date was chosen before running any scenario by a fixed rule: no BCP announcement or Portuguese event in the 5 days before and the 10 days after the shock, and, among the 28 days that satisfy this condition, the one with the lowest volatility of BCP and of the market around it (BCP volatility of 0.95% per day in the 20 previous days, half of its usual level). In this way the effect of the collars is not mixed with real news, while the rest of the market keeps its real behaviour.'
    Body 'Each configuration is replicated 30 times. Replication k of every configuration uses the same random seed (1000 + k), so configurations are compared on the same sequences of random events and differ only in the parameters under study. The replications must be run one at a time: when they were run in parallel (first baseline run), they wrote to the same output file at the same time and about 1% of the lines were damaged; the baseline was therefore repeated.'
    Body 'As for the stylised facts, the indicators of the scenarios were fixed and committed to the project repository before any scenario was run (docs/KPIS_CENARIOS.md, 25 November 2026), so that they could not be adjusted to the results ({T:kpiDefs}). There is no regulatory definition of a crash. A crash is defined with the data of BCP as a maximum fall on the day of the shock larger than the information shock (-10%) plus the intraday fall that BCP exceeded on only 1% of the days of 2023–2024 (7.44%, from the daily low relative to the previous close), that is, a fall below -17.44%; the 95% block-bootstrap interval of this threshold is -19.60% to -15.21%. As a robustness check the probability of a crash is also computed with the 95th percentile (-14.52%), and the excess fall of each run is reported, which does not depend on any threshold. A conclusion on the probability of a crash is considered robust only if it has the same direction with both thresholds and with the excess fall.'
    Tbl 'Indicators of the scenarios, fixed before the runs' @('Group', 'Indicator', 'Definition') @(
        @('Stability', 'Maximum fall', 'Lowest price on the day of the shock relative to the price just before the shock'),
        @('Stability', 'Excess fall', 'Maximum fall minus the -10% information shock'),
        @('Stability', 'Probability of a crash', 'Share of the 30 runs with a maximum fall below -17.44% (robustness: -14.52%), with a 95% Wilson interval'),
        @('Stability', 'Close and 5-day return', 'Return from the price before the shock to the close of the shock day and of the 5th day after'),
        @('Stability', 'Half-recovery time', 'Minutes from the minimum until the price recovers half of the fall (within 5 days)'),
        @('Liquidity', 'Spread', 'Mean relative spread outside reservations, on the shock day and in the 5 following days'),
        @('Liquidity', 'Volume', 'Shares traded on the shock day and in the 5 following days'),
        @('Liquidity', 'Time without continuous trading', 'Minutes in reservation and number of reservations on the shock day')
    ) @(2.4, 4.2, 9.4) 'kpiDefs'
    Body 'For each configuration the mean of each indicator is reported with its 95% confidence interval. Configurations with collars are compared with the same configuration without collars by the difference of the means, its 95% interval and a Welch t-test (unequal variances); the number of crashes is compared with Fisher exact test. The multi-criteria analysis normalises each indicator between 0 and 1 over all collar configurations, averages the stability indicators (maximum fall, probability of a crash, half-recovery time) and the liquidity indicators (spread, volume, time in reservation), and weights stability and liquidity 50/50, with 70/30 and 30/70 as robustness checks; a configuration is recommended only if it ranks first with the three weights.'
}

$content['Large Shock with and without Collars: Results'] = {
    Body 'With 13 trend followers, the calibrated share of the model, the -10% shock produces a maximum fall of about -15.5% on the day of the shock, that is, about 5.5 points more than the information justifies, and the price closes the day about 10.8% lower, in line with the new fundamental value ({T:res2}). The current collars of Euronext Lisbon (3% dynamic, 8% static) stop continuous trading for about 10 minutes on the day of the shock but hardly change the outcome: the maximum fall is 0.3 points smaller, a difference that is not statistically significant (p = 0.38), and the number of crashes goes from 3 to 2 out of 30 (p = 1.0). The spread and the traded volume are the same with and without collars.'
    Tbl 'Large shock (-10%) with and without the current collars, 13 trend followers (30 runs each)' @('Indicator', 'Without collars', 'With collars', 'Difference [95% CI]', 'p') @(
        @('Maximum fall on the shock day', '-15.75%', '-15.42%', '+0.33 pp [-0.4, +1.1]', '0.38'),
        @('Crashes (below -17.44%)', '3 / 30', '2 / 30', '-', '1.00 (Fisher)'),
        @('Crashes, robustness (below -14.52%)', '25 / 30', '25 / 30', '-', '1.00 (Fisher)'),
        @('Return at the close of the shock day', '-10.75%', '-10.85%', '-0.10 pp', '0.80'),
        @('Minutes in reservation on the shock day', '0', '10.3', '+10.3', '< 0.001'),
        @('Spread on the shock day', '0.11%', '0.11%', '0.00 pp', '0.79'),
        @('Volume on the shock day (million shares)', '4.80', '4.75', '-1.2%', '0.82')
    ) @(5.4, 2.4, 2.4, 3.6, 2.2) 'res2'
    Body 'The half-recovery time is not informative in this scenario. It measures the recovery of half of the total fall, which includes the -10% of the news: that part is justified by the information and permanent, so the price only gets back above -7.9% by chance, after one to three days on average (1 512 and 1 299 minutes, difference not significant, p = 0.51). This is a limitation of how the indicator was defined before the runs; it is kept as defined and interpreted with this caveat.'
}

$content['Shock with a High Share of Trend Followers: Results'] = {
    Body 'With 60 trend followers and herding (a trend follower panics after receiving between 1 and 4 sell signals from its neighbours, herdThreshold), the same -10% shock turns into a crash in most runs: without collars the maximum fall is between -21% and -27% and between 80% and 100% of the runs fall below -17.44%, against 10% with 13 trend followers ({T:res3}). The share of trend followers is therefore the main driver of the probability and size of crashes (research question 1). The effect of the herding threshold is not monotonic: the smallest fall occurs with the most sensitive herding (one signal), possibly because panic is triggered earlier and spread over time; the separate roles of the number of trend followers and of herding are analysed with the grid of collar configurations, reservation durations and numbers of trend followers (Collar Thresholds and Reservation Duration).'
    Tbl 'Shock with 60 trend followers and herding, with and without the current collars (30 runs each)' @('Sell signals for panic', 'Maximum fall without / with collars', 'Difference [95% CI], p', 'Crashes without / with collars', 'Minutes in reservation with collars') @(
        @('1', '-21.1% / -19.4%', '+1.7 pp [-0.4, +3.7], p = 0.11', '24 / 17 of 30 (p = 0.10)', '45'),
        @('2', '-26.8% / -22.1%', '+4.7 pp [+2.7, +6.8], p < 0.001', '30 / 24 of 30 (p = 0.02)', '72'),
        @('3', '-26.0% / -22.7%', '+3.3 pp [+1.3, +5.2], p = 0.001', '29 / 29 of 30 (p = 1.0)', '94'),
        @('4', '-24.7% / -22.2%', '+2.5 pp [+0.1, +4.9], p = 0.04', '27 / 26 of 30 (p = 1.0)', '97')
    ) @(2.6, 3.4, 4.0, 3.4, 2.6) 'res3'
    Body 'In this market the collars matter. They reduce the maximum fall by 2.5 to 4.7 points when two or more sell signals are needed for panic (p < 0.05), at the cost of 72 to 97 minutes without continuous trading on the day of the shock. The reduction in the probability of a crash is significant only with two signals and the main threshold (from 30 to 24 runs out of 30, p = 0.02); with the robustness threshold it disappears (30 and 29 runs), so by the rule fixed before the runs it is not a robust result. The spread and the volume do not differ significantly between the two cases. In every configuration the return at the close of the shock day is the same with and without collars (p > 0.5): the collars cut the excess fall caused by panic within the day, but they do not change the level at which the price settles, which reflects the information. Because the reservations stop trading, the half-recovery time is longer with collars (by 6 to 12 minutes, p = 0.008 to 0.05 with two to four signals).'
}

$content['Baseline Scenario: Results'] = {
    Body 'The baseline runs the same 120 days without the large shock and serves as the reference for the shock scenarios. On the reference day (day 60, 5 September 2024) the largest intraday fall is 1.1% on average, no run comes close to the crash thresholds (0 out of 30 with both thresholds), the price closes 0.9% higher, the relative spread is 0.10% and 4.5 million shares are traded; with the current collars there is no reservation on that day. These values describe an ordinary trading day of the model and confirm that the crashes of the shock scenarios are caused by the shock and the reaction of the agents, not by the background information. The baseline was also used for the one-minute comparison with BCP (Section 6.3).'
}

$content['Optimal Thresholds: Results'] = {
    Body 'The thresholds of the collars were varied in two markets: the calibrated market with 13 trend followers (dynamic threshold 2% to 5% and static threshold 6% to 12%, 16 combinations) and the market with 60 trend followers and herding (two sell signals for panic), where the collars had a significant effect (static threshold 6% to 12%, dynamic threshold fixed at 3%). In both cases each combination is compared with the same market without collars and the same random seeds. In the calibrated market the dynamic threshold had no effect at all: for a given static threshold the results are practically identical with a dynamic threshold of 2% or 5%. The dynamic collar compares each trade with the previous one, and in the model the price falls through many small steps after the shock, so no single trade moves the price by 2% or more. Only the static collar, measured against the reference price, stops the fall.'
    Body 'In the calibrated market no combination changes the maximum fall significantly (between -15.2% and -16.0%, against -15.8% without collars; all p > 0.1) or the number of crashes (p >= 0.24); the only significant effect is the cost, from 3 minutes in reservation with a static threshold of 12% to 16 minutes with 6%. The multi-criteria analysis finds no configuration that ranks first with the three weights, because it orders differences that are within the noise of the replications. In a market like the calibrated one the choice of the thresholds therefore matters little.'
    Body 'With herding the static threshold matters and shows a clear trade-off ({T:resThr}): the tighter the threshold, the smaller the fall and the longer the market is stopped. All four thresholds reduce the maximum fall significantly (by 3.8 to 6.6 points, p < 0.001), and the 6% and 8% thresholds also reduce the number of crashes with the main threshold (p = 0.01 and 0.02), although not with the robustness threshold. The spread is slightly lower with collars (0.134% to 0.139% against 0.141%) and the volume does not differ significantly. The return at the close of the shock day does not differ significantly from the case without collars in any configuration.'
    Tbl 'Static collar threshold in the market with herding (60 trend followers, two sell signals; dynamic threshold 3%; 30 runs each)' @('Static threshold', 'Maximum fall', 'Difference to no collars [95% CI]', 'Crashes (-17.44%)', 'Minutes in reservation', 'Rank 50/50, 70/30, 30/70') @(
        @('No collars', '-26.8%', '-', '30 / 30', '0', '5, 5, 5'),
        @('6%', '-20.2%', '+6.6 pp [+4.8, +8.5]', '23 / 30', '115', '1, 1, 1'),
        @('8% (current)', '-22.1%', '+4.7 pp [+2.8, +6.7]', '24 / 30', '72', '2, 2, 3'),
        @('10%', '-23.0%', '+3.8 pp [+2.0, +5.5]', '29 / 30', '45', '3, 3, 4'),
        @('12%', '-22.6%', '+4.2 pp [+2.3, +6.0]', '26 / 30', '29', '4, 4, 2')
    ) @(2.4, 2.2, 3.6, 2.4, 2.4, 3.0) 'resThr'
    Body 'Among these thresholds, the static threshold of 6% ranks first with the three weights of the multi-criteria analysis, and having no collars ranks last with every weight. When the collar sets and reservation durations of the grid (Collar Thresholds and Reservation Duration) are added to the same market, no configuration is first with all three weights: the tight static thresholds (5% and 6%) lead with the weights 50/50 and 70/30, and the 12% threshold is second with 30/70. This ranking should be read with care. The liquidity criterion averages three indicators normalised between 0 and 1, and the 6% threshold has the best spread and the highest volume although the differences are small (0.007 points of spread) and the difference in volume is not significant, while it has by far the longest time without continuous trading (almost two hours on the day of the shock). A decision maker who gives more weight to the time without trading than the equal weights of the analysis would prefer a wider threshold: 12% keeps about two thirds of the reduction of the fall obtained with 6% with a quarter of the time in reservation. The trade-off in {T:resThr} is therefore the main result for the decision, and the multi-criteria ranking one way of reading it.'
}

$content['Sensitivity Analysis: Results'] = {
    Body 'To check whether the effect of the collars depends on the parameters that could not be observed, the large-shock scenario was repeated with each of the most influential parameters identified in Section 6.3 at -50% and +50% of its calibrated value, one at a time, with and without the current collars ({T:resSens}).'
    Tbl 'Sensitivity of the effect of the current collars (13 trend followers, -10% shock, 30 runs each)' @('Parameter', 'Value', 'Maximum fall without / with collars', 'Difference, p', 'Crashes without / with collars') @(
        @('Calibrated values', '-', '-15.8% / -15.4%', '+0.3 pp, 0.38', '3 / 2'),
        @('Order lifetime (orderLife)', '17.5 min', '-16.3% / -15.9%', '+0.4 pp, 0.39', '10 / 5'),
        @('', '52.5 min', '-15.5% / -15.4%', '+0.1 pp, 0.78', '3 / 3'),
        @('Number of trend followers', '7', '-13.4% / -13.5%', '-0.1 pp, 0.72', '0 / 0'),
        @('', '20', '-18.3% / -17.5%', '+0.7 pp, 0.08', '21 / 17'),
        @('Trend threshold (trendThreshold)', '0.25%', '-16.3% / -16.4%', '0.0 pp, 0.96', '10 / 8'),
        @('', '0.75%', '-15.6% / -15.6%', '0.0 pp, 0.96', '4 / 3'),
        @('Number of noise traders', '15', '-17.3% / -17.0%', '+0.3 pp, 0.52', '16 / 11'),
        @('', '45', '-14.5% / -14.1%', '+0.4 pp, 0.35', '2 / 1'),
        @('Order rate of noise traders (noiseRate)', '0.5 per min', '-17.2% / -16.7%', '+0.5 pp, 0.26', '14 / 9'),
        @('', '1.5 per min', '-15.0% / -13.9%', '+1.0 pp, 0.01', '2 / 0')
    ) @(4.4, 1.8, 3.8, 2.8, 3.2) 'resSens'
    Body 'The effect of the collars does not change sign in any case: it is small, between -0.1 and +1.0 points, and significant only with the highest order rate of the noise traders (+1.0 point, p = 0.01), as with the calibrated values, so the conclusion for the calibrated market does not depend on these parameters. What changes strongly is the risk of a crash itself. With 20 trend followers instead of 13, 21 of the 30 runs without collars fall below -17.44%, and with 15 noise traders instead of 30, or with noise traders sending half as many orders, a less liquid market, 16 and 14 of the 30 runs do; with 7 trend followers, 45 noise traders or a higher order rate crashes almost disappear. This confirms that the share of trend followers and the liquidity provided by other investors are the main drivers of crashes in the model (research question 1). The sensitivity analysis was run in the calibrated market; whether the larger effect of the collars under herding is equally robust to these parameters was not tested within the project.'
}

$content['Discussion and Interpretation of Results'] = {
    Body 'The experiments give a consistent picture of what the volatility interruptions of Euronext Lisbon do in the model. After a large negative information shock the price falls in two parts: the part justified by the information (-10%), which is permanent and sets the level at which the price closes the day, and an excess fall caused by the way the investors react within the day. The collars act only on the second part. In every scenario the return at the close of the shock day is the same with and without collars, while the maximum intraday fall can be significantly smaller with them. In other words, the collars do not prevent the market from incorporating bad news; they limit the overshooting that panic and trend following add to it. This is the behaviour expected from a well-designed circuit breaker, and it is obtained here without being imposed on the model.'
    Body 'How much the collars help depends on the market they act on. In the calibrated market, with 13 trend followers, the excess fall is about 5.5 points, crashes are rare and the collars change little: no configuration of thresholds reduces the maximum fall or the probability of a crash significantly, and the only clear effect is the cost of 3 to 16 minutes without continuous trading. This result is robust: in the sensitivity analysis the effect of the current collars never changes sign and stays small (at most 1 point) for the five influential parameters tested. As the share of trend followers grows, the excess fall grows to 11 to 17 points, crashes become almost certain and the collars matter: from 30 trend followers onwards almost every collar set reduces the maximum fall significantly, by up to 8 points with the tightest static threshold. The collars are therefore most useful precisely in the fragile markets in which crashes happen.'
    Body 'The mechanism that does the work is the static collar. The dynamic collar, which compares each trade with the previous one, was never binding, because after the shock the price falls through many small trades rather than a few large jumps. The static collar, measured against the reference price, stops the fall once it has gone far enough and gives the market a pause, the reservation and reopening auction, in which liquidity can return. The cost of this pause is time without continuous trading, not a worse spread or a lower volume: the collars changed the spread by at most 0.01 points (in most cases lowering it) and reduced the traded volume significantly in only one of more than forty comparisons. The trade-off for the regulator is therefore between the size of the fall and the time the market is stopped, and it is clearly visible with herding: a static threshold of 6% reduces the fall the most but stops the market for almost two hours on the day of the shock, whereas 12% keeps about two thirds of the reduction with a quarter of the time in reservation.'
    Body 'These conclusions are conditional on the model. The investors never run out of cash or shares, so forced selling, which deepens real crashes, is absent; the model is about four times noisier than BCP within the minute, although far below the collar thresholds; and the sensitivity analysis was run in the calibrated market only. Because the scenarios share the same assumptions and the same random seeds, the comparisons between configurations are more reliable than the absolute size of the falls, which should be read as indicative. The grid that crosses the collar sets, the duration of the short reservation and the number of trend followers confirms these conclusions across markets and shows that the duration of the reservation matters much less than the thresholds: a 10-minute reservation adds stopped time without a consistent gain in stability.'
}

$content['Final Considerations'] = {
    Body 'This project built in AnyLogic an agent-based model of the BCP share traded under the rules of Euronext Lisbon, calibrated it on the real information and the statistical properties of BCP in 2023–2024, validated it out of sample in a calm period (2025–2026) and in a crisis (2020–2021), and used it to evaluate the volatility interruptions. The model reproduces the seven daily stylised facts of BCP in the calibration period and the five price facts in both test periods, with its parameters fixed.'
    Body 'RQ1 (share of trend followers and herding). The share of trend followers is the main driver of crashes in the model. After a -10% shock and without collars, the maximum fall deepens from -15.0% with 10 trend followers to -26.8% with 60, and the share of runs below the crash threshold jumps from 2 of 30 with 10 trend followers (3 of 30 with the calibrated 13) to 21 of 30 with 20 and to 27 to 30 of 30 from 30 trend followers onwards. Lower liquidity from other investors has a similar effect (16 of 30 runs with half the noise traders and 14 of 30 when they send half as many orders). The effect of the herding threshold is not monotonic.'
    Body 'RQ2 (collar thresholds and reservation durations). In a market like the calibrated one, no combination of thresholds reduces the maximum fall significantly; the current collars of Euronext Lisbon cost about 10 minutes without continuous trading on the day of the shock and change the fall by 0.3 points. As the share of trend followers grows, the collars reduce the fall significantly, by up to 8 points, without harming the spread or the volume; the static threshold is what matters, the dynamic threshold had no effect in the range tested, and a reservation of 10 minutes instead of 3 adds stopped time without a consistent gain.'
    Body 'RQ3 (thresholds that minimise the fall for an acceptable loss of volume). Traded volume was not significantly reduced by any configuration, so the binding cost is the time without continuous trading. In the most fragile market (60 trend followers with herding) the tight static thresholds of 5% and 6% minimise the maximum fall (-19.5% and -20.2% against -26.8% without collars) and lead the multi-criteria analysis with the weights 50/50 and 70/30, at the cost of two to two and a half hours in reservation on the day of the shock; a threshold of 12% gives about two thirds of that reduction with about half an hour in reservation. No configuration is first with all three weights in every market, so the choice depends on the weight given to the time without trading.'
    Body 'The recommendation that follows from the model, and not an investment advice, is to keep the volatility interruptions and to rely on the static collar: in normal conditions it costs little, and in fragile, herding-driven markets it limits the excess fall substantially. A short reservation (3 minutes) is preferable to a longer one. Whether the static threshold for a stock like BCP should be tighter than the current 8% depends on how much time without continuous trading the market operator is willing to accept; the model quantifies this trade-off rather than resolving it, and the result should be confirmed with a model that includes forced selling before it is used for policy.'
}

$content['Collar Thresholds and Reservation Duration: Results'] = {
    Body 'The grid crosses the four collar sets (none; 2% dynamic and 5% static; 3% and 8%, the current values; 5% and 10%), two durations of the short reservation (3 and 10 minutes) and six numbers of trend followers (10 to 60), with 30 runs each (1 440 runs). It answers both research questions at once ({T:resGrid}).'
    Tbl 'Maximum fall on the shock day by number of trend followers and collar set (reservation of 3 minutes; 30 runs each)' @('Trend followers', 'No collars', '2% / 5%', '3% / 8% (current)', '5% / 10%', 'Crashes without collars', 'Minutes in reservation (5% / 8% / 10%)') @(
        @('10', '-15.0%', '-14.3%*', '-14.6%', '-14.4%', '2 / 30', '22 / 7 / 3'),
        @('20', '-18.3%', '-17.3%*', '-17.5%', '-18.1%', '21 / 30', '37 / 16 / 8'),
        @('30', '-20.7%', '-17.8%*', '-19.1%*', '-19.3%*', '27 / 30', '59 / 22 / 16'),
        @('40', '-22.6%', '-18.6%*', '-20.9%*', '-20.2%*', '29 / 30', '98 / 40 / 20'),
        @('50', '-23.4%', '-19.4%*', '-22.5%', '-21.2%*', '27 / 30', '128 / 59 / 31'),
        @('60', '-26.8%', '-19.5%*', '-22.1%*', '-22.9%*', '30 / 30', '155 / 72 / 44')
    ) @(2.2, 2.0, 2.0, 2.4, 2.0, 2.4, 3.6) 'resGrid'
    Body 'The share of trend followers drives the size and the probability of crashes (research question 1): without collars the maximum fall deepens steadily from -15.0% with 10 trend followers to -26.8% with 60, and the share of runs below -17.44% jumps from 2 of 30 with 10 trend followers to 21 of 30 with 20 and to 27 to 30 of 30 from 30 trend followers onwards. Crashes in the model are therefore not a gradual consequence of the shock: they appear once trend followers are a sizeable part of the market.'
    Body 'The effect of the collars grows with the fragility of the market. With 10 or 20 trend followers only the tightest set (5% static) reduces the fall significantly, by about 1 point (marked with * in {T:resGrid}, Welch test, p < 0.05); from 30 trend followers onwards almost every set does, and the reduction reaches 4 to 8 points with 60 trend followers. The tightest set gives the largest reduction in every market (2.8 to 7.3 points from 30 trend followers onwards, p < 0.001) and the largest and most frequent significant reductions in the number of crashes (for example from 30 to 18 of 30 with 60 trend followers, p < 0.001; from 29 to 22 with 40, p = 0.03), but it also stops the market the longest, from 22 minutes with 10 trend followers to more than two and a half hours with 60. The wider sets cost less time and give smaller reductions. The return at the close of the shock day does not change with the collars, and the costs in spread and volume are negligible: the significant differences in spread are all reductions of less than 0.01 points, and the volume of the shock day falls significantly in only one of the 36 comparisons (by 8%, with 10 trend followers and the tightest set) and rises in two.'
    Body 'The duration of the short reservation matters much less than the thresholds. Comparing 10 with 3 minutes for the same collar set and number of trend followers, the maximum fall differs significantly in only 2 of the 18 cases (by 1.4 and 1.5 points, both in favour of 10 minutes), while the time without continuous trading on the shock day is always longer with 10 minutes, by 7 to 40 minutes. A longer reservation therefore adds stopped time without a consistent gain in stability.'
    Body 'The multi-criteria analysis, applied separately to each number of trend followers, does not give one configuration that ranks first with the three weights in every market. With the rule fixed before the runs, a robust first place appears only with 50 trend followers (5% static, 10-minute reservation); in the other markets the first place changes with the weights, usually between the tight 5% static set and the current or the widest set. In the variant without the half-recovery time (not fixed before the runs), the 5% static set with a 10-minute reservation is first with the three weights in four of the six markets. As in the threshold experiment, the advantage of the 10-minute reservation in the liquidity criterion comes from very small differences in spread amplified by the normalisation, while it costs clearly more time without trading, so the ranking between the two durations should not be over-interpreted.'
}
